    {
      "id": "be-s12-calculus-optimization-c2",
      "heading": "First and second order conditions",
      "explanation_md": "**Step 1: differentiate the profit function.**\n\ndπ/dQ = dTR/dQ - dTC/dQ\n\n**Step 2: recognize the marginal values.**\n\n**MR = dTR/dQ** and **MC = dTC/dQ**\n\n**Decision rule:**\n\n- **First order condition:** profit is maximized when **dπ/dQ = 0**, or equivalently **Marginal Revenue = Marginal Cost**.\n- **Second order condition:** **d²π/dQ² < 0**. The slide describes this as the second-order condition for a maximum being satisfied.\n\nYou need **both**: the first tells you where the flat point is, the second confirms it is a maximum.",
      "example_ids": [],
      "key_terms": [
        {
          "term": "First order condition",
          "meaning": "Profit is maximized when dπ/dQ = 0, i.e. MR = MC.",
          "source_ref": "be--session-1-and-2#56"
        },
        {
          "term": "Second order condition",
          "meaning": "d²π/dQ² < 0 for a maximum.",
          "source_ref": "be--session-1-and-2#56"
        }
      ],
      "exam_line": "First order condition: dπ/dQ = 0, i.e. MR = MC. Second order condition: d²π/dQ² < 0.",
      "clarification": null,
      "check_questions": [
