    {
      "id": "be-s12-calculus-optimization-c3",
      "heading": "Worked example: TR = 50Q - Q² and TC = 10Q",
      "explanation_md": "**Question:** a firm's **TR = 50Q - Q²** and **TC = 10Q**. Find the profit-maximizing quantity and the maximum profit.\n\n**Step 1: profit function.** π(Q) = TR(Q) - TC(Q) = **50Q - Q² - 10Q**\n\n**Step 2: first order condition.** Differentiate and set equal to zero: dπ/dQ = dTR/dQ - dTC/dQ = 0, so MR - MC = 0, so **MR = MC**.\n\n- MR = 50 - 2Q (the slide prints this as \"50Q - 2Q\"; differentiating 50Q - Q² gives 50 - 2Q, which is what produces the answer)\n- MC = 10\n- MR = MC: 50 - 2Q = 10, so **Q = 20**\n\n**Step 3: second order condition.** d²π/dQ² = -2 < 0, so **profits are maximised at Q = 20**.\n\n**Step 4: maximum profit.** 50Q - Q² - 10Q = 50 × 20 - 20 × 20 - 10 × 20 = 1,000 - 400 - 200 = **$400**",
      "example_ids": [
        "be-ex-calculus-example"
      ],
      "key_terms": [],
      "exam_line": "TR = 50Q - Q², TC = 10Q: MR = MC gives Q = 20; d²π/dQ² = -2 < 0; maximum profit = $400.",
